<GuideLayout
  title="Zero-Defect Inspection System for Activewear Production"
  type="QUALITY CONTROL"
  description="How activewear factories reduce defects through fabric inspection, inline QC, measurement checks, final inspection and packing control."
>
  <p>No factory can honestly promise that defects never happen. A zero-defect system means building process controls that catch problems early and prevent repeat issues from reaching the buyer.</p>

  <h2>Inspection Stages</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Incoming fabric inspection for color, defects and stretch stability.</li>
    <li>Pre-production sample approval before bulk cutting.</li>
    <li>Inline checks during sewing and assembly.</li>
    <li>Measurement checks across sizes and colorways.</li>
    <li>Final inspection before packing and shipment.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Common Defects to Watch</h2>
  <p>Activewear quality issues often include twisted seams, uneven waistbands, poor stitch tension, shade variation, weak logo adhesion, transparent fabric and inconsistent sizing.</p>

  <h2>Buyer Recommendation</h2>
  <p>Ask your manufacturer to explain the inspection stages before placing a bulk order. A supplier that can describe the QC workflow clearly is usually easier to manage long term.</p>
</GuideLayout>
